<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.6.4: Sorting Polynomials by Factoring Methods</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01686</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.6.4: Sorting Polynomials by Factoring Methods</md:title>
    <md:uuid>031624f3-68e2-4c28-873c-2a915ccaaac0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Your teacher will display six different polynomials. Sort these polynomials by whether you would factor them
      using the difference of squares or the perfect square trinomial method.</para>
            <para>Draw a table similar to this on a sheet of paper. Record your results. If a polynomial requires both methods,
      place it in both columns.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Difference of Squares Method</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Perfect Square Trinomial Method</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
             
             
             
          </entry>
                  <entry>
             
             
             
          </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answers:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Difference of Squares Method</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Perfect Square Trinomial Method</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
          \(27q^2 − 3\)
          \(4p^2 − 100\)
          \(x^2 − 10x + 25 − y^2\)
          \(x^2 + 12x + 36 − y^2\)
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          \(16x^2 − 72x + 81\)
          \(36y^2 + 12y + 1\)
          \(x^2 − 10x + 25 − y^2\)
          \(x^2 + 12x + 36 − y^2\)
        </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <note>
        <para>Why Should I Care?</para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_09_09_01.png"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
        <para>Ozzie Gomez and Juan Casava own a store called Plant Shop Chicago. They use polynomials everyday to make sure the operations of their small business go smoothly.</para>
        <para>One of the most important parts of running a business is understanding where your money is going. You need to know how much you are spending and how much you are making. For example, Ozzie Gomez and Juan Cassava can use polynomials to calculate how many different plants they can buy and how much money they can make off of each sale.</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
